# Trivy ignore file — accepted HIGH advisories (0 CRITICAL) in the runtime images.
# Reviewed 2026-06-22 (see docs/production-readiness/risk-register.md PR-014). The Trivy gate is
# BLOCKING (deploy-checks.yml exit-code 1); every entry is an explicit, justified acceptance and the
# gate still FAILS on any NEW package/advisory not listed here (allowlist model, like .secrets.baseline).
#
# OS/debian base layers scan 0 HIGH/CRITICAL. Root-cause fixes landed (2026-06-21):
#   - next: upgraded 14 -> 15.5.x — FIXED (the 5 next advisories CVE-2026-44573/44578 + GHSA-8h8q/h25m/
#     q4gf are gone), so they are intentionally NOT listed (the gate fails if they reappear).
#   - glob / tmp (dev-only build tooling via jest / @nestjs/cli): the runtime images now do a CLEAN
#     prod-only install (`rm -rf node_modules && npm ci --omit=dev` in apps/{api,web}/Dockerfile), so
#     these are ABSENT from the image — verified gone, NOT listed.
#   - picomatch: after the NestJS 11 CLI update, the API dev-tooling path uses picomatch 4.0.4; with
#     `--omit=dev`, `npm ls picomatch` is empty across workspaces — i.e. NO picomatch is an installed
#     (lockfile) dependency of either runtime image. See the picomatch acceptance below for the
#     vendored re-appearance.
#
# Transitive/vendored advisory families that genuinely persist in the prod images and are accepted:
#
# ── picomatch 4.0.3 — CVE-2026-33671 — Regular-Expression Denial of Service via crafted extglob ──
# HIGH, 0 CRITICAL, no RCE (availability-only). NOT an installed dependency: `npm ls picomatch
# --omit=dev` is empty in both workspaces. Trivy detects it from a VENDORED copy bundled inside
# build tooling — `next/dist/compiled/picomatch` (web, Next 15.5.19) and @angular-devkit/core (api) —
# used only at build time for glob matching, never to match untrusted runtime input. There is no
# overridable lockfile entry (it is vendored, not a resolvable dep); the only upstream fix is Next 16
# (a major upgrade requiring full frontend re-validation). FOLLOW-UP: drop this entry when the
# frontend moves to Next 16 (vendors picomatch >= 4.0.4) — the allowlist will then fail if it reappears.
#
#
# ── multer 2.1.1 (transitive PROD dep) — Denial of Service (malformed/deeply-nested/dropped uploads) ──
# HIGH, 0 CRITICAL, no RCE. @nestjs/platform-express@11.1.27 still depends on multer 2.1.1; npm audit
# marks multer vulnerable below 2.2.0. Root/workspace npm overrides to 2.2.0 were tested and did not
# affect the workspace lockfile/reified tree (`npm ls` still shows 2.1.1), while `npm ci` stays strict.
# Mitigated meanwhile: this app exposes no file-upload controller, and the public API sits behind the
# PR-011 WAF/rate-limits. Availability-only. FOLLOW-UP: upgrade @nestjs/platform-express once it depends
# on multer >=2.2.0, or switch to a package-manager override that reifies 2.2.0 and is proven by CI.
# ── sigstore 3.1.0 (npm-CLI-bundled) — CVE-2026-48815 — certificateOIDs constraints not enforced ──
# HIGH, 0 CRITICAL. Found ONLY at usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/sigstore — the copy
# vendored inside the npm CLI that ships with the node:22-slim BASE image, in both api and web
# runtime images. It is not an app dependency (`npm ls sigstore` is empty in all workspaces) and the
# api container runs `node dist/...` (npm never executes); web's `npm run start` only shells out to
# `next start` and performs no registry/signature operations, which is the vulnerable surface
# (sigstore verifies npm provenance attestations during install/audit — never done at runtime).
# Fix arrives when the base image bumps npm to one bundling sigstore >= 4.1.1. FOLLOW-UP: strip the
# npm/corepack/yarn CLIs from the runtime stages (api can today; web needs its CMD moved off
# `npm run start` first) — then this entry can be dropped and the gate will fail if it reappears.
